  "worked_solution": "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $4x+10y=20,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+10y & =20 \\\\ 10y & =-4x+20 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{4}{10}x+\\frac{20}{10} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{5}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $-\\frac{2}{5}.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=\\frac{5}{2}x-4$ has a slope of $\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{2}{5})\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=\\frac{5}{2}x-4$ is perpendicular to the given line.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
